description:
Site: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/chat.rs:120-136 (handle_chat_set create branch).

The current code:

    let existing_chats: Vec<Chat>;
    let mut known_direct_contact_ids: HashSet<String>;
    if has_direct_create {
        let (chats, _) = backend.get_objects::<Chat>(...).await...?;
        known_direct_contact_ids = chats.iter().filter(...).filter_map(...).collect();
        existing_chats = chats;
    } else {
        existing_chats = Vec::new();
        known_direct_contact_ids = HashSet::new();
    }

declares two bindings with type annotation, then conditionally assigns them in both branches. Rust is expression-oriented; this idiom is what C-shaped imperative code does. An idiomatic rewrite uses 'if as expression' returning a tuple:

    let (existing_chats, mut known_direct_contact_ids): (Vec<Chat>, HashSet<String>) =
        if has_direct_create {
            let (chats, _) = backend.get_objects::<Chat>(...).await...?;
            let known = chats.iter().filter(...).filter_map(...).collect();
            (chats, known)
        } else {
            (Vec::new(), HashSet::new())
        };

What goes wrong if left: a contributor adding a third branch (or a new field that also needs the same conditional init) has to remember to update both declaration AND both branches. The expression form makes the invariant 'these two are populated together' structurally obvious; the declare-then-assign form lets future-edits drift the two halves apart without compiler help.

This is the only spot in the file that uses this pattern — every other conditional-init in chat.rs is an expression. The local inconsistency suggests this branch grew the second variable late and was patched rather than rewritten.

Scope: P2 clippy (readability); not a correctness bug.
